---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Methodology — How the data works — lolperform"
  description="How lolperform samples ranked matches, scores champions, assigns tiers, and handles sample size. No black boxes."
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-2xl space-y-8 py-6 text-md leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <header class="space-y-1">
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold tracking-widest text-accent uppercase">Methodology</p>
      <h1>How these numbers are made.</h1>
      <p>Most stats sites hide this page. Here it's the point: you should be able to check our work.</p>
    </header>

    <section class="space-y-2">
      <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
      <p>
        Riot's API has no "tier list" endpoint. Like every stats site, we build it ourselves: we
        crawl ranked Solo/Duo ladders (the <code class="text-text-primary">league-v4</code> and
        <code class="text-text-primary">match-v5</code> APIs), then aggregate the matches. Champion
        art and item icons come from Riot's Data Dragon CDN.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-2">
      <h2>It's a sample, and we say so</h2>
      <p>
        We don't claim to read every game. We sample ranked matches across <strong
          class="text-text-primary">NA, EUW, and KR</strong> on the current patch, weighted toward the
        larger rank populations so the sample isn't skewed toward the apex few. We're a sampled
        dataset, not a full-ladder one like the largest sites — which is exactly why <strong
          class="text-text-primary">every statistic shows its sample size</strong>. A win rate over 40
        games is not the same as one over 40,000, and the UI never lets you forget it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-2">
      <h2>Ranking: Wilson lower bound, not raw win rate</h2>
      <p>
        Champions are ordered by the <strong class="text-text-primary">Wilson score interval lower
        bound</strong>, not their raw win rate. This penalises small samples: a 60% win rate over 10
        games will not outrank a 53% win rate over 5,000. It's the same math used to sort “best
        rated” lists — reward certainty, not luck.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-2">
      <h2>Tiers</h2>
      <p>
        The grade is deliberately simple and explainable: a champion is placed on a fine
        <strong class="text-text-primary">S+ → D−</strong> scale by its win rate. The base letter sets
        the colour and the row; the <strong class="text-text-primary">+ / −</strong> shows where it
        sits inside that band.
      </p>
      <ul class="list-inside list-disc space-y-1 text-text-muted">
        <li><span class="text-tier-s">S</span> tier ≥ 51.5% win rate (S+ ≥ 53.5%, S ≥ 52.5%, S− ≥ 51.5%)</li>
        <li><span class="text-tier-a">A</span> tier ≥ 50% &nbsp;·&nbsp; <span class="text-tier-b">B</span> tier ≥ 48.5%</li>
        <li><span class="text-tier-c">C</span> tier ≥ 46% &nbsp;·&nbsp; <span class="text-tier-d">D</span> tier below that</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Bands are calibrated to the real ranked win-rate distribution (champions cluster near 50%, the
        strongest reach ~52–53%). A champion must also clear <strong class="text-text-primary">200
        games</strong> before it can be tiered above D− — so a tiny lucky sample never shows up as S+.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-2">
      <h2>Confidence</h2>
      <p>Sample size maps to a visible treatment on every stat:</p>
      <ul class="list-inside list-disc space-y-1 text-text-muted">
        <li><strong class="text-text-primary">High</strong> (≥ 1,000 games): solid.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-text-primary">Medium</strong> (≥ 200): dimmed, dashed underline.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-text-primary">Low</strong> (≥ 30): heavily dimmed, ⚠ chip.</li>
        <li>Below 30: treated as low and often hidden — we won't show a number we don't trust.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-2">
      <h2>How fresh it is</h2>
      <p>
        A scheduled job checks Riot's patch version a few times a day. When a new patch ships, it
        re-crawls and reloads the dataset automatically — so the tiers track the live meta without
        anyone pressing a button.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-2 border-t border-border-subtle pt-6 text-sm text-text-muted">
      <p>
        lolperform.com isn't endorsed by or affiliated with Riot Games. We display only aggregate
        statistics and never expose individual players' match histories.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>
